# Every row a rule can examine, with its verdict. One row per (rule, item examined): which rule
# looked at it, which layer of which filing it is, whether it breaks the rule, and a sentence
# saying what it says.
#
# One relation, two views
#   The violations table and the coverage table both read this relation. The violations table is
#   `WHERE violates`; the coverage table is `GROUP BY rule`. Because they read one relation, a
#   coverage figure cannot be about a different population from the rule it reports on. Written
#   twice, two populations drift apart, and a coverage count of four rows where the rule examines
#   one would report a thin rule as well covered.
#
# The most useful thing here to run on its own
#   An empty violations table is the good outcome, and it tells a reader nothing. This shows every
#   rule at work on real rows: what it looked at, what each row said, and why it passed. That is
#   the only way to see that a rule is doing something rather than merely finding nothing.
#
# A rule that examines nothing still appears
#   It appears with a NULL verdict, which is why every check starts by joining checks/roster.sqlc.
#   A check that only filtered its population would vanish from a `GROUP BY` when the population
#   was empty, so the verdict that matters most, that a rule examined nothing, is the one a
#   grouping cannot produce. The roster row exists before the population does, so the rule reports
#   that it ran even when it had nothing to look at.
#
# What every check returns
#   One plain SELECT, with no leading WITH, returning exactly `(rule, filing, layer, violates,
#   detail)`, and `violates` NULL only on the row that says a rule examined nothing. The checks are
#   joined with UNION ALL, so a check that needs recursion puts the walk in its own relation under
#   composition/ and joins it, where the walk also runs on its own.
-- the conformance rules XSD 1.0 cannot reach, one file each; checks/roster.sqlc is the list.
:union(ALL checks/fit_disagrees.sqlc,
           checks/shares_do_not_sum.sqlc,
           checks/stated_quantity_is_not_the_magnitude.sqlc,
           checks/nobody_named_as_unserved.sqlc,
           checks/exposure_unaccounted.sqlc,
           checks/share_exceeds_slack.sqlc,
           checks/slack_unit_mismatch.sqlc,
           checks/quantum_unit_mismatch.sqlc,
           checks/nameplate_not_a_multiple.sqlc,
           checks/draw_exceeds_the_supply.sqlc,
           checks/clearance_with_unserved.sqlc,
           checks/unresolved_part.sqlc,
           checks/jagged_layer.sqlc,
           checks/layers_move_together.sqlc,
           checks/coupling_does_not_attenuate.sqlc,
           checks/narrows_a_point_value.sqlc,
           checks/range_says_no_range.sqlc,
           checks/bound_fell_with_no_range.sqlc,
           checks/identity_does_not_compute_the_claim.sqlc,
           checks/window_lost_or_summed.sqlc,
           checks/derived_slack_over_a_window.sqlc,
           checks/window_not_applicable_on_a_rate.sqlc,
           checks/window_size_not_applicable.sqlc,
           checks/elimination_not_applicable_with_parts.sqlc,
           checks/fusion_sum_disagrees.sqlc,
           checks/local_part_dangles.sqlc,
           checks/denied_remainder_is_not_contradicted.sqlc,
           checks/one_part_fusion_alters_its_part.sqlc,
           checks/conversion_cycle_does_not_close.sqlc,
           checks/unit_crossing_without_a_factor.sqlc,
           checks/regime_crossing_without_a_citation.sqlc,
           checks/part_regime_disagrees.sqlc)
